<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luxembourgers</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Luxembourgers are the people and citizens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, a small nation whose national identity developed after the grand duchy became independent in the nineteenth century. Their national language is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourgish">Luxembourgish</a>, used alongside French and German, and their motto, 'We want to remain what we are', expresses resistance to absorption by larger neighbours, notably during the German occupation of 1940–1944. Nearly half of the country's residents today are foreign nationals.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luxembourgers.jpg" alt="Luxembourgers" width="320" height="166" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Allice Hunter · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Luxembourger_Diaspora_in_the_World.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Luxembourgers are a Germanic ethnic group native to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, where they make up around half of the population. Most live in the Grand Duchy, a small landlocked country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, and are of Celtic or Gallo-Roman and Germanic, Frankish, origin. They share the culture of Luxembourg and speak <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourgish">Luxembourgish</a>, a West Germanic language, which is the only native language of Luxembourgers as taught by parents, though nearly all learn French and German in school.</p><p>Like the Austrians and some aspects of the Swiss, Luxembourgers were historically considered a regional sub-group of ethnic Germans and regarded themselves as such until the collapse of the German Confederation. Luxembourg became independent, while remaining in personal union with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-london-1839">Treaty of London</a> of 1839. The personal union proved short-lived and was dissolved by agreement in 1890. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourgish">Luxembourgish</a> law all citizens of the Grand Duchy are Luxembourgers, although a distinct Germanic ethnolinguistic identification is vocally espoused and promoted. The corresponding adjective is Luxembourgish.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>There is a relatively large diaspora, in the neighbouring countries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, mostly because of the three Partitions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, which brought former territories into each of them. The largest contingent overseas lives in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Others went to the medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> during the first phase of German eastward settlement in the 12th century, and later during the Modern Age. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvanian-saxons">Transylvanian Saxons</a>, whose dialect is very close to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourgish">Luxembourgish</a>, and partly the Banat Swabians descend from these settlers, as do part of the ancestors of the Zipser Germans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spis">Zips</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> and their descendants in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maramures">Maramureș</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukovina">Bukovina</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>.</p><p>The first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> settlers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> came from the Rhine-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a> valley region, and so also from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>. They were invited in the late 12th century by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geza-ii-of-hungary">Géza II of Hungary</a> to develop, fortify and defend southern and south-eastern Transylvania against invading peoples such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a>. In 2007 Luxembourg City shared the title of European Capital of Culture with the Romanian town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sibiu">Sibiu</a>, one of the most important historical centres of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvanian-saxons">Transylvanian Saxons</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Luxembourgers” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Nazi regime annexed it in all but name under the Gauleiter Gustav Simon, imposed German law and in 1942 introduced conscription of young <b>Luxembourgers</b> into the Wehrmacht, which provoked a general strike that was brutally repressed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Grand Duchy of Luxembourg · Military</a></li></ul></section>
